  "title": "Świętokrzyskie Mountains — the Oldest Mountains in Poland",
  "lead": "The Świętokrzyskie Mountains are the oldest mountains in Poland. Bare rocks, monastery on Łysa Góra, caves. Slow travel through the Świętokrzyskie Mountains.",
  "body": "<h2>Why it's worth it</h2><p>The Świętokrzyskie Mountains are the oldest mountains in Poland — their age is estimated at about 500 million years. They lie in the Świętokrzyskie voivodeship, in central-eastern Poland. The highest peak is Łysica (614 m), but the symbol of the region is Łysa Góra (595 m) with a monastery on the summit. The Świętokrzyskie Mountains are low but very diverse: bare rocks, boulder fields, fir forests, scree, and caves. Add to that cultural heritage: the monastery on Łysa Góra, which was a pilgrimage site as early as the Middle Ages, and the sanctuary at Święty Krzyż.</p><p>Slow travel through the Świętokrzyskie Mountains is a journey through a landscape that surprises with its diversity. The region has mountains, monasteries, caves, castles, and villages — all within a few dozen kilometres. It is worth spending 2–3 days here to truly feel this landscape. The best way to explore the region is on foot or by car — there are about 300 km of hiking trails, including the famous Edmund Massalski Trail. Public transport from Kielce is good (buses, trains).</p><h2>What to see</h2><p>The Świętokrzyskie Mountains are divided into three main parts: the Łysogóry Range (centre), the Jeleniów Range (east), and the Chęciny Range (west). Exploring the mountains takes 2–3 days.</p><h3>Łysogóry Range</h3><ul><li><strong>Łysa Góra</strong> — a peak with a monastery, one of the most important sanctuaries in Poland. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Boulder fields</strong> — rock rubble on the slopes of Łysogóry, unique in Poland. Worth it for views.</li><li><strong>Święty Krzyż</strong> — a sanctuary on Łysa Góra, with relics of the Holy Cross. Worth it for history.</li></ul><h3>Jeleniów Range</h3><ul><li><strong>Jeleniowska Góra</strong> — a peak in the Jeleniów Range (536 m). Worth it for views.</li><li><strong>Krzyżtopór Castle</strong> — ruins of a castle in Ujazd, one of the largest in Poland. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Ujazd village</strong> — a village with Krzyżtopór Castle. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h3>Chęciny Range</h3><ul><li><strong>Chęciny Castle</strong> — ruins of a castle on a rock, with a viewpoint. Worth it for architecture.</li><li><strong>Raj Cave</strong> — a karst cave, one of the most beautiful in Poland. Worth it for nature.</li><li><strong>Chęciny</strong> — a small town at the foot of the castle, with a market square and church. Worth it for architecture.</li></ul><h2>When to go</h2><ul><li><strong>May and June</strong> — the best time for slow travel. Warm, green, few tourists.</li><li><strong>July–August</strong> — trail season. Worth it for hiking, but crowded on Łysa Góra.</li><li><strong>September–October</strong> — second best. Autumn colours, fewer people, mushroom picking in the forests.</li><li><strong>November–March</strong> — the region is sleepy in winter, many attractions closed. Worth it for those seeking silence.</li></ul><h2>Getting there</h2><ul><li><strong>By car:</strong> ~2 hours from Warsaw (S7), ~2 hours from Kraków (DK73), ~2 hours from Łódź (S7).</li><li><strong>By train:</strong> Kielce, Chęciny, Ostrowiec Świętokrzyski have railway stations.</li><li><strong>By bus:</strong> PKS serves most towns and villages.</li></ul><p>The Świętokrzyskie Mountains are best explored by car or on foot — the region is vast, and public transport between villages can be sparse. Leave the car at the car park in Nowa Słupia or Chęciny.</p><h2>How much time you need</h2><p><strong>A weekend (2 days)</strong> is the minimum — Łysa Góra, Chęciny Castle.</p><p><strong>Three days</strong> is realistic for a first visit. Łysa Góra, Chęciny Castle, Raj Cave, Krzyżtopór.</p><p><strong>A week</strong> lets you explore the whole region — from Kielce to Ostrowiec, with numerous stops.</p><h2>What to eat and where to sleep</h2><ul><li><strong>Pierogi with cabbage and mushrooms</strong> — a Świętokrzyskie classic.</li><li><strong>Świętokrzyskie kwaśnica</strong> — sour cabbage soup with white sausage.</li><li><strong>Goat cheeses</strong> — from local farms, available in village shops.</li><li><strong>Beer from Świętokrzyskie breweries</strong> — several craft breweries in the region.</li></ul><p>Accommodation: Kielce (city, more options), Nowa Słupia (village, guesthouses), Chęciny (small town, guesthouses), Święty Krzyż (monastery, guesthouses).</p><h2>Practical tips</h2><ul><li><strong>Guided tours</strong> — the region has rich history, take a guide at Święty Krzyż.</li><li><strong>Raj Cave</strong> — daily entry limits apply. Book tickets in advance.</li><li><strong>Shopping</strong> — buy honey, cheese, and cold cuts directly from farmers.</li><li><strong>Edmund Massalski Trail</strong> — the main trail of the Świętokrzyskie Mountains, about 80 km. Worth hiking in 3–4 days.</li><li><strong>Weather</strong> — weather can be changeable, even in July bring a jacket.</li><li><strong>Signal</strong> — good throughout the region, including in the mountains.</li></ul>",
  "tip": "Climb Łysa Góra early in the morning — the best time to see the monastery without crowds and with good light for photos.",
